{0 Router}

The router is a machine whose states are the routes. Its snapshot is the
current route with its data, and the URL is that snapshot printed. A link
press, a navigation action, the back and forward buttons and a typed URL all
reach the router as the event [Url_changed]. See {!Silica.Router} and
{!Silica.Route}.

{1:routes Routes}

The routes are a plain variant, as the states of any machine. A constructor
carries the data of its route: its path parameters and its typed query. Each
{!Silica.Router.val-route} gives three things for one constructor: the
{!Silica.Route.t} that reads and writes the URL, a function that makes the
route from the data, and a function that gives the data back, or [None] for
another route.

The deriver [[@@deriving routes]] of [silica.ppx] writes these routes for you.
Put the path of each constructor in a [[@route ...]] attribute. For a type
[route] it makes [route_routes] (for a type [t], [routes]), in the order of
the constructors. A constant constructor has the data [()]; a constructor with
two or more arguments has a tuple, so its path uses [/+]. A constructor with no
[[@route]] is a compile error. The two functions come from one constructor, so
they always agree. When you write {!Silica.Router.val-route} by hand, the
checker finds a pair that does not agree: [Check.app] checks
[parse (print route) = Ok route] for every route it reaches
([route-round-trip]).

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_books.mlx[
open Silica

type search = { text : string; page : int }

type route =
  | Home [@route Route.root]
  | Book of int [@route Route.(s "books" / int)]
  | Search of search
      [@route
        Route.(
          s "search"
          +? map_query
               (fun (text, page) -> { text; page })
               (fun { text; page } -> (text, page))
               (string_query "q" ~default:"" &? int_query "page" ~default:1)
        )]
  | Not_found of string [@route Route.rest]
[@@deriving routes]

let routes =
  Router.routes
    ~not_found:(function Not_found _ -> true | _ -> false)
    route_routes

let title = function
  | Home ->
      "Books"
  | Book id ->
      "Book " ^ string_of_int id
  | Search { text; _ } ->
      "Search for " ^ text
  | Not_found _ ->
      "Not found"

let page : route -> Router.event Node.t = function
  | Home ->
      <p>"Pick a book."</p>
  | Book id ->
      <p>(Node.text ("This is book " ^ string_of_int id ^ "."))</p>
  | Search { text; page } ->
      <p>(Node.text (Printf.sprintf "Results for %s, page %d." text page))</p>
  | Not_found path ->
      <p>(Node.text ("Nothing at " ^ path ^ "."))</p>

let render current =
  <div>
    <nav>
      (Router.link ~current routes Home [] [ Node.text "Home" ])
      (Router.link ~current routes (Book 7) [] [ Node.text "Book 7" ])
      (Router.link ~current routes
         (Search { text = "ocaml"; page = 1 })
         []
         [ Node.text "Search" ]
      )
    </nav>
    (Node.element "main"
       [ Attr.bool "data-silica-route-focus" true ]
       [ page current ]
    )
  </div>

let app url = Router.make routes ~title ~render url
]}

The parts of a route:

{t
| Pattern | Matches |
|---------|---------|
| [Route.root] | the empty path, [/] |
| [Route.s "books"] | one fixed segment |
| [Route.int], [Route.string] | one segment as data; [int] does not match ["007"] or ["+7"] |
| [Route.rest] | the whole remaining path; a route with it, placed last, takes every other path |
| [a / b], [a // b], [a /+ b] | a fixed segment then a pattern, a pattern then a fixed segment, two patterns |
| [path +? query], [Route.with_query path query] | a path with a typed query |
}

A query has a name and a default for each value: [string_query],
[int_query], [bool_query], [choice_query] or [query] with your own parse and
print. [&?] joins two values, and [map_query] turns them into a record. A
value that does not parse gives the default, and a default value is not
printed, so one route has one URL:

{@ocaml[
# let url = Silica.Url.of_string "/search?page=x&q=ocaml";;
val url : Silica.Url.t =
  {Silica.Url.path = ["search"]; query = [("page", "x"); ("q", "ocaml")];
   hash = None}
# let route = Silica.Router.parse Doc_books.routes url;;
val route : (Doc_books.route, string) result =
  Ok (Doc_books.Search {Doc_books.text = "ocaml"; page = 1})
# Silica.Url.to_string
    (Silica.Router.print Doc_books.routes (Result.get_ok route));;
- : string = "/search?q=ocaml"
# Silica.Router.name Doc_books.routes (Doc_books.Book 7);;
- : string = "/books/:int"
]}

The router tries the routes in the order of the list. The last route must
match every path, so a bad path goes to it.

[parse] rejects a path outside the [base], and a path that no route matches.
[Router.make] raises [Failure] when [parse] rejects the first URL. When
[parse] rejects the URL of a [Url_changed], the route does not change and the
router raises {!Silica.Action.Reload}: the host loads the URL from the server,
which decides the answer. When [parse] rejects the URL of a [Preload], the
router does nothing.

{1:app The router component}

[Router.routes] lists the routes, with these options:

- [base]: the path that every URL of the app starts with, such as ["/app"].
  [parse] needs it and takes it off; [print] puts it first.
- [not_found]: the routes that {!Silica.Router.is_not_found} names. The
  server uses it for the status 404.
- [reenter]: the routes whose page starts again when only the data of the
  route changes. Any other route keeps its page, which gets the new data as
  new props.
- [preload]: the Remote calls of each route (see {{!section-preload}Preload}).

[Router.make routes ~render url] is the component for the root of the app.
[render] shows the page of the current route. In an app, each page is a
component node with its own machine; the pages of this example are plain
nodes. The options of [Router.make]:

- [title]: the document title of a route. The router raises
  {!Silica.Action.Set_title} at the start and at each change of route or data,
  before the actions of the page, so a page that sets its own title wins. A
  server render writes it in the [<title>] of the first HTML.
- [guard]: a function that runs before a URL change takes effect (see
  {{!section-guard}Guard}).
- [exit]: actions to run when a route is left.

The browser mounts the app with {!Silica_browser.mount_app}. A test mounts it
with {!Silica_memory.mount_app}:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount_app ~url:"/books/7" Doc_books.app;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.title page;;
- : string = "Book 7"
# Silica_memory.press (Silica_memory.by_text page "Search");;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.url page, Silica_memory.title page;;
- : string * string = ("/search?q=ocaml", "Search for ocaml")
# Silica_memory.back page;;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.url page, Silica_memory.title page;;
- : string * string = ("/books/7", "Book 7")
]}

{1:links Links and navigation}

{!Silica.Router.link} makes an [a] element with [href] and
[data-silica-link]. The browser turns a press into a history entry with no
page load. A press with a modifier key or another button, and a press on a
link with [target] or [download], are left to the browser. The options of a
link:

- [current]: the route now. The link gets [aria-current="page"] when its path
  equals the current path, and [data-active] when its path is a prefix of the
  current path, as for the link of a parent page. A link to the root ([/], or
  the base path) is [data-active] only on the root, since every path starts
  with it. The query does not count.
- [hash]: the URL has this hash, and the browser scrolls to the element with
  that id after the paint. The router ignores the hash.
- [preload]: when the host fetches the calls of the route of the link.

{!Silica.Router.link_replace} replaces the history entry instead. Use it
for a link that changes only the query, such as a filter.

A machine navigates with actions: [Router.navigate routes (Book 7)] pushes
an entry and [Router.replace] replaces it. Both take [hash]. A navigation to
the same printed URL does nothing.

{1:layouts Layouts and nested pages}

There is no tree of routes. A layout is a component at a fixed place in
[render], such as a header. The instance tree keeps it by its position and the
id of its machine, so it keeps its state when the page changes. A child page that needs
the data of its parent carries it in its own route data. [data-active] marks
the link of the parent page.

{1:guard Guard}

[guard ~from ~target] decides each URL change before it takes effect. [from]
is the current route, or [None] at the start. The answer is:

- [Allow].
- [Redirect route]: go to that route instead, and replace the URL. At the
  start, the app starts in the other route. A server render answers it with a
  redirect, status 303.
- [Cancel]: stay. The router raises {!Silica.Action.Revert_navigation}, so the
  URL and the position in the history go back, also after back or forward.

Use the guard for rules that depend on routes only, such as "go to the login
page".

{1:block Unsaved changes}

A page that holds a draft holds the block: {!Silica.Host.block} in the
[during] list of the snapshot that holds the draft. While the list holds it,
every navigation (a link press, a navigation action, back and forward) stops
before the URL changes, and reaches the page as a {!Silica.Nav.t}. The page
asks the user, then sends [Action.Resume nav] to let it happen. While a block is
held, closing or reloading the tab asks the user through the browser.

The block starts and stops with the snapshot that holds it, so it cannot
stay after the draft is saved. The edit page of the example app holds one
while its draft is not empty ([example/app/shared/example_edit.mlx]).

{1:preload Preload}

[Router.routes ~preload] gives the Remote calls that the page of a route makes
when it starts. A link starts them early:

- [Intent] (the default): when the pointer enters the link or the link gets
  focus.
- [Viewport]: when the link first enters the viewport.
- [Never].

The runtime keeps a successful answer for 30 seconds, for one use. The page
takes it when it makes the same call with the same input, so it shows with no
loading state. A link whose route has no calls has no preload attribute.

{1:lazy Lazy routes}

A lazy route keeps the code of its page out of the first JavaScript file: the
browser loads it the first time the route shows, or earlier, when a link to
the route preloads. The site does it for its component pages.

The code is a {!Silica.Chunk.t}. The view code is shared by the native server
and the browser, but only Melange has a dynamic import, so each platform
library of the app has one small module with the same name. The native one
has the value at once:

From [site/app/native/site_lazy.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
let component_page = Silica.Chunk.ready Site_component_page.page
]}

The browser one imports it. esbuild with [--splitting] then puts
[Site_component_page], and the code only it uses, in a file of its own:

From [site/app/melange/site_lazy.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
let component_page =
  Silica_browser.import (fun () -> Js.import Site_component_page.page)
]}

The page shows the chunk with {!Silica.Node.val-chunk}, with a view for the
time it loads:

From [site/app/shared/site_app.mlx]:
{@ocaml skip[
        (Node.chunk Site_lazy.component_page ~loading:component_loading
           (fun page -> page slug
]}

The routes name the lazy routes, so that a link to one preloads; the app root
names their code. The code cannot be named in [routes], because the page links
to routes and so depends on them:

From [site/app/shared/site_app.mlx]:
{@ocaml skip[
let chunks = function
  | Component _ ->
      [ Chunk.Any Site_lazy.component_page ]
]}

From [site/app/shared/site_routes.ml]:
{@ocaml skip[
    ~chunked:(function Component _ -> true | _ -> false)
]}

What happens:

- On the server the chunk is ready: the HTML has the page.
- In the browser, the first paint waits until the chunks of the first view are
  loaded, so hydration adopts the server HTML with the code of its page. Timers,
  calls and resources start at once. Give the page's shared chunks to the server
  as [preload] (see {!page-server.render}) so they load with the entry.
- After the first paint, a route whose code is not loaded shows [loading] until
  it is. A link to a [chunked] route loads the code on intent, like the calls
  of [~preload], so a click rarely sees [loading].
- A load that fails raises {!Silica.Node.exception-Chunk_failed} in the view:
  the nearest {!Silica.Node.val-boundary} shows its fallback, and its reset
  loads again. With no boundary, the error page's "Try again" does.
- The checker and the memory platform use the native module: the chunk is
  always ready.

{1:server Status and redirects on the server}

The server render gives the status from the current route, for example 404
when [is_not_found] is [true]. A page can set its own status with
{!Silica.Action.Set_status}; the last one wins. A [Navigate] or [Replace] during
a server render, such as a guard [Redirect] at the first URL, makes the answer
a redirect: status 303 with a [location] header. See {!page-server.render}.

{1:focus Focus, title and scroll}

After a push, back or forward to another path, the browser moves focus, with
no scroll, to the first element with [data-silica-route-focus], or else to the
root. A polite live region then reads the new document title, so a screen
reader user knows that the page changed. A [Focus] action after the change
wins.

The browser keeps the scroll position of each history entry, also across a
reload. After a push it scrolls to the element of the hash, or to the top when
the path changed. A push that changes only the query, and a replace, do not
scroll.

{1:check Check the routes}

{!Check.app} explores the router with a simulated history. Besides the
built-in properties, it checks that every route reads back from its own URL,
and that every link in a view is canonical and does not go to the not-found
route. See {!page-checker.app}.

{1:not-built What the router does not do}

The design compared the router with TanStack Router, React Router and the
Next.js App Router. These features are left out on purpose:

- Optional segments: two routes give the same result, and each route keeps
  one printed URL.
- Relative links and links from path strings: a link is made from a typed
  route and its data.
- Data loaders in the router: Remote data stays in machines, where the
  checker sees it. Preload removes the wait.
- History state: the data of a route is its URL, and Local state lives as
  long as its component.
- View transitions: the browser runs the change of the page in a later
  callback, which breaks the paint that focus and handlers need.
